analysisWe need to produce an assert statement for the comment:

```java
// @@@ If the index is valid (greater than or equal to 0 and less than the initial size of the set), then the result is the element that was at that index before the operation.
```

We are in method:

```java
public E remove(final int index) {
    final E obj = setOrder.remove(index);
    remove(obj);
    return obj;
    // @@@ If the index is valid (greater than or equal to 0 and less than the initial size of the set), then the result is the element that was at that index before the operation.
}
```

We need to write an assert that captures this specification.

Key points: "initial size of the set" means size before removal. So we need to capture old size using \old(this.size()) perhaps. Also "result is the element that was at that index before the operation". So \result equals the element that was at that index before removal. That's \old(this.get(index)) maybe, but we need to be careful: if index is valid (0 <= index < old size). After removal, the element at index may be different, but we need to assert that the returned element equals the element that used to be at that index before removal: \result == \old(this.get(index)). However, get may be defined to retrieve element at position; we can use this.get(index) if method available. Yes, ListOrderedSet has get(int). So we can refer to this.get(index). Use \old(this.get(index)). Also need to restrict to valid index: index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size()). So the assert statement: assert (index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size())) => \result.equals(\old(this.get(index)));

But need to consider null elements: E may be any type; equality maybe using Objects.equals. In spec examples they used .equals for objects (like this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i)))). For return value equality they used == for primitives, .equals for objects. Since E is generic, we can use \result.equals(\old(this.get(index))) but if result could be null, calling .equals on null would be problematic. Better use Objects.equals(\result, \old(this.get(index))) but not allowed? In JML we can use \result == \old(this.get(index)) if reference equality? Usually spec uses == for object identity, but they used .equals earlier. In example they used this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i))) which is method call, not spec operator. So we could write \result.equals(\old(this.get(index))) assuming non-null. Might be okay.

Thus final assert:

```java
assert (index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size())) => \result.equals(\old(this.get(index)));
```

Make sure parentheses around implication. That's it.

Return final answer in code block.assistantfinal<code>assert (index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size())) => \result.equals(\old(this.get(index)));</code>
